Step 2: We are also given the area of the lower base and upper base of the frustum:

- Lower base: 225π cm^2
- Upper base: 25π cm^2

We can calculate the radii of both bases using the formula for the area of a circle: \begin{align*} \text{Area} &= \pi \cdot \text{radius}^2 \\\ text{Lower base radius (r)} &= \sqrt{\frac{225\pi}{\pi}} \\\ &= 15 \text{ cm} \\ \text{Upper base radius (R)} &= \sqrt{\frac{25\pi}{\pi}} \\\ &= 5 \text{ cm} \end{align*}

Step 3: Now that we have the radii of the bases, we can calculate the ratio of the larger base to the smaller base:

\begin{align*} Ratio &= \frac{R}{r} \\\ &= \frac{5}{15} \\\ &= \frac{1}{15} \end{align*}

Step 4: We know the height of the frustum (h) is 24 cm and the ratio of the larger base to the smaller base is $\frac{1}{15}$. We want to find the height of the smaller cone that was cut off (denoted as $x$). Since we are dealing with similar triangles (it is similar to a similar triangle problem involving base-to-height ratios), we can set up the proportion:

\begin{equation} \frac{x}{h} = \frac{r}{R} \end{equation}

Plugging in the values we know, we can solve for $x$:

\begin{align*} \frac{x}{24} &= \frac{1}{15} \\\ x &= \frac{24}{15} \\\ &= 1.6 \text{ cm} \end{align*}

So the altitude of the smaller cone that was cut off is \boxed{1.6} cm.

